    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Transportation</span> is the physical movement of goods and persons from one place (point of production) to another (point of consumption). It is one of the most important marketing functions because it creates <span class="learn-keyword">place utility</span> — goods become useful to consumers only when they are available where they are needed.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Transportation</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Transportation bridges the gap between producers and consumers who are often separated by distance. Without an efficient transport system, goods produced in one location (e.g., yams from Benue) cannot reach markets in another location (e.g., Lagos) in good condition and at the right time.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Types of Transportation</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Examples</th><th>Best suited for</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Land</td><td>Road (trucks, trailers), Rail</td><td>Short-to-medium distance, bulky and perishable goods</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Water</td><td>Rivers, Lakes, Sea/Ocean shipping</td><td>Very bulky, heavy, non-perishable goods over long distances</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Air</td><td>Cargo planes</td><td>Perishable, urgent, high-value, low-bulk goods</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Pipeline</td><td>Crude oil, gas, water pipelines</td><td>Liquids and gases over fixed routes</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Importance of Transportation in Marketing</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Transportation creates <strong>place utility</strong> (moving goods to where they are demanded), widens the market for producers, enables specialization and division of labour by region, stabilizes prices by moving surplus goods to areas of scarcity, and supports the growth of trade and industry.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Problems of Transportation in Nigeria</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Common problems include poor road networks and potholes, inadequate rail infrastructure, high cost of fuel and vehicle maintenance, traffic congestion, insecurity (armed robbery, kidnapping) on highways, seasonal flooding, and insufficient storage facilities at ports.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Solutions to Transportation Problems</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Solutions include government investment in road and rail infrastructure, provision of security along trade routes, subsidizing fuel for commercial transporters, developing inland waterways, and encouraging private sector investment in logistics.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Examiners like to test which mode of transport suits a given product. Perishables (fish, vegetables) need fast transport (road/air); bulky non-perishables (crude oil, minerals) suit water or pipeline.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Transportation moves goods from producers to consumers, creating place utility. The four types are land, water, air, and pipeline, each suited to different goods. Nigeria\'s transport system faces infrastructural and security challenges that raise the cost of marketing.\n</div>\n  ',
