    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Warehousing</span> is the marketing function of storing goods safely from the time of production until they are needed by consumers. It creates <span class="learn-keyword">time utility</span> — making goods available at the time they are wanted, rather than only when they are produced.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Warehousing</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A warehouse is a building or facility used to store raw materials, semi-finished, or finished goods before they are distributed to the market. Warehousing bridges the time gap between production (which may be seasonal, e.g., harvest) and consumption (which is continuous throughout the year).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Types of Warehouses</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Private warehouse</td><td>Owned and used by a single business for its own goods</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Public warehouse</td><td>Owned by an individual/company and rented out to any trader for a fee</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Bonded warehouse</td><td>Government-licensed store for imported goods on which duty has not yet been paid</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Cooperative warehouse</td><td>Jointly owned and managed by members of a cooperative society</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>State (government) warehouse</td><td>Owned and operated by government, often for strategic reserves (e.g., grains)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Functions and Importance of Warehousing</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Warehousing protects goods from damage, theft, and weather; balances seasonal supply with continuous demand; enables bulk-breaking (dividing large shipments into smaller units); stabilizes prices by holding stock until needed; and supports grading, packaging, and quality control before distribution.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Don\'t confuse a bonded warehouse with a private warehouse. A bonded warehouse specifically holds imported goods until customs duty is paid — this is a common trick question.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Warehousing stores goods to bridge the gap between production and consumption, creating time utility. The five main types are private, public, bonded, cooperative, and state warehouses, each serving different storage needs in marketing.\n</div>\n  ',
