    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Markets do not simply appear on their own — they have a <span class="learn-keyword">structure</span> (how buyers and sellers are organized) and are often supported by <span class="learn-keyword">market unions</span> or associations that regulate and coordinate the sale of goods.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Market Structure</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Market structure refers to the way a market is organized based on the number of buyers and sellers, the nature of the product, and the degree of competition. Understanding structure helps marketers know how prices are set and how much control sellers have.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Types of Market Structures</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Structure</th><th>Key Feature</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Perfect competition</td><td>Many buyers and sellers, identical products, no single seller controls price</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Monopoly</td><td>A single seller controls the entire market for a product with no close substitute</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Oligopoly</td><td>A few large sellers dominate the market (e.g., telecom companies)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Monopolistic competition</td><td>Many sellers offering slightly differentiated products (e.g., branded soaps)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Market Unions and Associations</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Market unions (also called traders\' associations) are groups formed by traders in a market to protect their common interests. Examples include commodity associations (e.g., Yam Sellers Association) and umbrella market unions that manage a whole market (e.g., a central market union).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Functions of Market Unions</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Market unions set and enforce trading rules and standards, negotiate with government/landlords over levies and market space, resolve disputes among traders, regulate prices to prevent unhealthy undercutting, organize members for bulk purchasing, and represent traders\' interests to authorities.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Do not confuse \'market structure\' (the competitive arrangement of buyers/sellers) with \'market union\' (an organization of traders). They are related but distinct concepts.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Market structure describes how competitive a market is (perfect competition, monopoly, oligopoly, monopolistic competition). Market unions are trader associations that regulate conduct, resolve disputes, and protect members\' interests in the distribution of goods.\n</div>\n  ',
