    term: 'First Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Marketing facilitators</span> (also called middlemen or intermediaries) are individuals or organizations that help move goods from producers to final consumers without necessarily taking ownership of the goods themselves in every case, or by taking temporary ownership to resell.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Facilitators</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Facilitators are agencies that assist in the exchange of goods between producers and consumers. They bridge the gap created by differences in location, time, and information between the two parties, making the flow of goods smoother and more efficient.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Types of Marketing Facilitators</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Agents</td><td>Represent buyers or sellers in negotiations without taking ownership of goods; earn commission</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Brokers</td><td>Bring buyers and sellers together for a single transaction; do not hold stock; paid a fee</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Wholesalers</td><td>Buy goods in bulk from producers and sell in smaller quantities to retailers</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Retailers</td><td>Buy from wholesalers/producers and sell directly to final consumers in small units</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Roles and Importance of Facilitators</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Facilitators reduce the number of direct contacts producers need with individual consumers, provide market information to both producers and consumers, break bulk into manageable units, provide storage and financing, and share the risk of unsold goods. Without them, most producers would find it very costly and time-consuming to reach every final consumer directly.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Differences between Agents and Brokers</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Agents usually have an ongoing relationship with the party they represent and can negotiate on their behalf continuously, while brokers are typically engaged for a single deal and their role ends once that transaction is complete.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> A common exam trick is distinguishing wholesalers (buy bulk, sell smaller quantities to retailers) from retailers (sell directly to final consumers). Both take ownership of goods, unlike agents and brokers.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Marketing facilitators — agents, brokers, wholesalers, and retailers — help move goods efficiently from producers to consumers. They reduce transaction costs, provide market information, break bulk, and share risks, making distribution far more efficient than direct producer-to-consumer sales.\n</div>\n  ',
