      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Successfully selling in foreign markets requires more than just a good product — it requires proper <span class="learn-keyword">organization</span>: the right internal structure, government support, and a clear method of entering the target market.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Organizational Structures for International Marketing</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Structure</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Export department</td><td>A dedicated unit within the company handling all export activities</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>International division</td><td>A separate division overseeing all foreign operations across multiple countries</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Global/regional structure</td><td>Company organized by world regions, with local managers for each region</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Roles of Government in International Marketing</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Government supports international marketing by negotiating favorable trade agreements, providing export incentives and subsidies, setting and enforcing quality/export standards, offering trade information and export promotion services (e.g., through agencies like the Nigerian Export Promotion Council), and maintaining diplomatic/trade relations with other countries.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Methods of Entry into International Markets</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Common entry methods include direct exporting (selling directly to foreign buyers), indirect exporting (using export agents/trading companies), licensing (allowing a foreign firm to use your brand/technology for a fee), franchising, joint ventures (partnering with a local foreign firm), and wholly owned foreign subsidiaries (setting up your own foreign branch/factory).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Entry methods form a "risk ladder": exporting is lowest risk/investment, while a wholly owned foreign subsidiary is highest risk but offers greatest control. Examiners like to test where each method sits on this scale.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Organizing for international marketing involves choosing the right internal structure (export department, international division, or global structure), leveraging government support, and selecting an appropriate market entry method — from exporting to full foreign investment.\n</div>\n  ',
