    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Just like living things, products go through stages from "birth" to "death" in the market. This journey is called the <span class="learn-keyword">Product Life Cycle (PLC)</span>, and understanding it helps marketers plan strategy at each stage.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Product Life Cycle</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The Product Life Cycle describes the stages a product passes through from its introduction into the market until it is eventually withdrawn (discontinued). Sales and profit levels typically change predictably across these stages.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. The Four Stages of PLC</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Characteristics</th><th>Typical Strategy</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Introduction</td><td>Low sales, high promotion costs, little/no profit</td><td>Heavy advertising to build awareness</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Growth</td><td>Rapidly rising sales and profits, more competitors enter</td><td>Improve product, expand distribution</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Maturity</td><td>Sales peak and level off, intense competition, profits may decline</td><td>Differentiate, defend market share, consider price cuts</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Decline</td><td>Sales and profits fall as new products/trends replace the old</td><td>Cut costs, harvest profits, or discontinue the product</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Importance of Understanding PLC</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Knowing which stage a product is in helps marketers decide when to invest more in promotion, when to adjust price, when to innovate/improve the product, and when to withdraw a product from the market before it becomes unprofitable.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember the order: Introduction → Growth → Maturity → Decline (sometimes abbreviated IGMD). Examiners often give a scenario and ask which stage it represents.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> The Product Life Cycle has four stages — Introduction, Growth, Maturity, and Decline — each with distinct sales, profit, and competitive characteristics, guiding marketers on which strategies to apply at each point in a product\'s life.\n</div>\n  ',
