    module: 'Product & Pricing Strategy',
    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Branding</span> and <span class="learn-keyword">packaging</span> help a product stand out on a crowded shelf, build customer trust, and protect the product until it reaches the final consumer.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Branding</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A brand is a name, term, symbol, design, or combination of these used to identify a seller\'s product and distinguish it from competitors. Branding is the process of creating a unique identity and image for a product in the minds of consumers.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Types of Branding</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Individual branding</td><td>Each product has its own unique brand name (different from the company name)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Family/umbrella branding</td><td>All products of a company are sold under one common brand name</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Manufacturer branding</td><td>Product is branded and marketed under the manufacturer\'s own name</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Private/store branding</td><td>Product is branded under the retailer\'s/store\'s own name</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Meaning and Types of Packaging</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Packaging is the process of designing and producing the container or wrapper for a product. It includes primary packaging (directly holding the product, e.g., a bottle), secondary packaging (outer wrap, e.g., a box), and transit/shipping packaging (for bulk transport, e.g., a carton).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Importance of Branding and Packaging</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Branding builds customer loyalty and trust, differentiates products from competitors, and allows premium pricing. Packaging protects the product from damage, provides essential product information, aids in convenient handling/storage, and serves as a silent salesman that attracts attention on the shelf.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Problems in Branding and Packaging</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Challenges include the high cost of developing strong brands and quality packaging, risk of counterfeiting/imitation of popular brands, environmental concerns over packaging waste, and the difficulty of changing negative brand perceptions once established.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Don\'t confuse a "brand name" (words/letters, e.g., Coca-Cola) with a "brand mark" (symbol/logo with no words, e.g., the Nike swoosh) — a common distinction tested in exams.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Branding gives a product a unique identity that builds trust and loyalty, while packaging protects the product and communicates information, also acting as a silent salesman. Both face challenges of cost, counterfeiting, and environmental concerns.\n</div>\n  ',
