    module: 'Distribution & Logistics',
    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  A <span class="learn-keyword">channel of distribution</span> is the path or route a product takes as it moves from the producer to the final consumer. Choosing the right channel affects cost, speed, and market reach.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Distribution Channels</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A distribution channel consists of all the individuals and organizations involved in moving a product from the point of production to the point of consumption, including any intermediaries used along the way.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Types of Distribution Channels</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Channel</th><th>Path</th><th>Best suited for</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Direct (Zero-level)</td><td>Producer → Consumer</td><td>Perishables, custom/farm goods, services</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>One-level</td><td>Producer → Retailer → Consumer</td><td>Moderate-volume consumer goods</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Two-level</td><td>Producer → Wholesaler → Retailer → Consumer</td><td>Mass-produced convenience goods</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Three-level</td><td>Producer → Agent → Wholesaler → Retailer → Consumer</td><td>Complex/international products needing specialized agents</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Advantages and Disadvantages of Distribution Channels</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Direct channels give producers full control and higher profit margins but limit market reach and require more resources to manage sales. Longer channels (with wholesalers/retailers) widen market reach and reduce the producer\'s workload but reduce the producer\'s control over final pricing and add extra cost/markup at each level.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Role of Intermediaries in Distribution Channels</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Intermediaries (wholesalers, retailers, agents) reduce the number of direct transactions needed, provide storage and local market knowledge, break bulk into consumer-sized units, and share the financial risk of unsold goods with the producer.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Learn to count the "levels" correctly: the number of levels equals the number of INTERMEDIARIES between producer and consumer. Direct = 0 intermediaries (zero-level).</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Distribution channels range from direct (producer to consumer) to multi-level channels involving agents, wholesalers, and retailers. Shorter channels offer more control but limited reach; longer channels widen reach through intermediaries but reduce control and add cost at each stage.\n</div>\n  ',
