    topic: 'Public Relations in Marketing',
    topicCode: 'SS2-MKT-20',
    module: 'Promotion & Communication',
    term: 'Third Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Public Relations (PR)</span> is the management of communication between an organization and its various publics to build a favorable image and maintain goodwill, often without directly paying for media coverage.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Public Relations</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Public relations involves deliberate, planned, and sustained efforts to establish and maintain mutual understanding and goodwill between an organization and the different groups (publics) it interacts with — customers, employees, government, media, and the general community.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Tools/Methods of Public Relations</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Press releases</td><td>Official statements sent to media announcing news about the company</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Sponsorships</td><td>Funding events, sports teams, or community programs to build goodwill</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR)</td><td>Community development projects (e.g., building schools, health outreach)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Press conferences</td><td>Organized meetings with journalists to share important company news</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Crisis management</td><td>Managing communication during a company scandal or emergency to protect reputation</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Importance of Public Relations</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">PR builds a positive, trustworthy company image, manages and protects reputation during crises, creates goodwill with the community and government, generates free/earned media coverage, and supports long-term brand credibility beyond paid advertising.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Difference between PR and Advertising</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Unlike advertising (which is paid and fully controlled by the company), public relations often relies on earned media coverage (e.g., a positive news story) that is not directly paid for, making it potentially more credible to the public but harder for the company to fully control the message.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> A very common exam question asks you to distinguish PR (unpaid/earned, image-focused) from advertising (paid, message-controlled). Know this distinction well.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Public relations manages an organization\'s relationship and image with its various publics through tools like press releases, sponsorships, CSR, and crisis management. Unlike paid advertising, PR often relies on earned media, building credibility and goodwill over the long term.\n</div>\n  ',
